      "side": 1,
      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] Suggestion: Writing to the SPI flash status registers unconditionally on every boot adds unnecessary overhead (SPI transactions) and could potentially wear out non-volatile bits on the flash chip. It is safer to only write back to the status register if the SRP bits were actually set.\n\n```c\n\tret \u003d flash_get_status(dev, \u0026sr1, \u0026sr2);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\treturn ret;\n\t}\n\n\tif ((sr1 \u0026 SPI_FLASH_SR1_SRP0) || (sr2 \u0026 SPI_FLASH_SR2_SRP1)) {\n\t\tsr1 \u0026\u003d ~SPI_FLASH_SR1_SRP0;\n\t\tsr2 \u0026\u003d ~SPI_FLASH_SR2_SRP1;\n\n\t\tret \u003d flash_set_status(dev, sr1, sr2);\n\t\tif (ret) {\n\t\t\treturn ret;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n```\n\nNote: If you apply this change, you will also need to update `test_init_status_register_write_failure` in `main.c` to pre-set an SRP bit so that the write path is actually triggered:\n```c\nZTEST_USER(cros_flash_rtkfp, test_init_status_register_write_failure)\n{\n\tfake_set_sr(SPI_FLASH_SR1_SRP0, 0);\n\tfake_ex_op_fail \u003d true;\n\tzassert_equal(cros_flash_init(cros_flash_dev), -EIO);\n}\n```\n[🔗](http://go/tj/f81d731e-cf41-49aa-a1ab-0b589e15710d)",
